1
00:00:20,537 --> 00:00:28,302
[SPEAKER_05]: So, this is the Chula Vedala Sutta, and I've shortened it a bit, just to make it con... yeah, make it concise.

2
00:00:29,543 --> 00:00:39,149
[SPEAKER_05]: There are many questions in it, and they are asked by a layman called Visakha, and he asks a nun, Damodina.

3
00:00:41,070 --> 00:00:48,635
[SPEAKER_05]: And the first question he asks is, in what sense does the Buddha refer to the term Sakaya?

4
00:00:49,604 --> 00:00:50,584
[SPEAKER_05]: Sakaya, sakaya.

5
00:00:50,725 --> 00:00:51,165
[SPEAKER_05]: What is that?

6
00:00:51,205 --> 00:00:51,745
[SPEAKER_05]: What does that mean?

7
00:00:52,865 --> 00:01:02,329
[SPEAKER_05]: And the translation for that is... well, there's many different types, but it's identity, identity view, personality view, or embodiment.

8
00:01:03,490 --> 00:01:07,512
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's person, identified, body.

9
00:01:10,373 --> 00:01:13,254
[SPEAKER_05]: So, what... in what sense does the Buddha refer to sakaya?

10
00:01:15,097 --> 00:01:19,619
[SPEAKER_05]: And the answer to that is the five assumed aggregates are referred to as sakāya.

11
00:01:19,799 --> 00:01:23,561
[SPEAKER_05]: That is form, feeling, perception, saṅkharas and consciousness.

12
00:01:27,023 --> 00:01:30,985
[SPEAKER_05]: So, what sense does the Buddha use the term sakāya?

13
00:01:31,325 --> 00:01:32,966
[SPEAKER_05]: What would your translation be for that?

14
00:01:34,867 --> 00:01:35,387
[SPEAKER_03]: What is that?

15
00:01:38,208 --> 00:01:43,051
[SPEAKER_05]: Personality... Because that is the five assumed aggregates.

16
00:01:44,648 --> 00:01:49,692
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's any notion of personality, any notion of identification with anything.

17
00:01:50,913 --> 00:02:01,983
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's why, as far as the extent of identification goes, as far as the extent of Sakaya goes, it's to the extent of the five aggregates.

18
00:02:02,443 --> 00:02:06,386
[SPEAKER_03]: You cannot be identified with anything else that's not within five aggregates.

19
00:02:07,547 --> 00:02:12,391
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, it's interesting to see that the person asking this is not asking what is myself.

20
00:02:13,876 --> 00:02:14,436
[SPEAKER_05]: What is the Self?

21
00:02:14,456 --> 00:02:15,077
[SPEAKER_05]: What is atta?

22
00:02:15,177 --> 00:02:15,877
[SPEAKER_05]: Atta, what is that?

23
00:02:16,318 --> 00:02:17,438
[SPEAKER_05]: He's asking what is Sakaya?

24
00:02:18,519 --> 00:02:19,399
[SPEAKER_03]: Right, right, right, right.

25
00:02:19,540 --> 00:02:21,981
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, well, means he already kind of understood the difference there.

26
00:02:23,542 --> 00:02:25,263
[SPEAKER_05]: So, what is this person?

27
00:02:26,384 --> 00:02:28,105
[SPEAKER_03]: It's that ownership.

28
00:02:29,205 --> 00:02:39,772
[SPEAKER_03]: See, you can't have... you can't have attavāda, you can't have the Self of you without underlying ownership, but you can still have the underlying ownership without a Self of you.

29
00:02:42,541 --> 00:02:50,405
[SPEAKER_03]: So, unquestioned ownership of your five aggregates is how and why the Atavāda exists for people.

30
00:02:51,465 --> 00:02:57,008
[SPEAKER_03]: It is why they think in terms of I was in the past, I will be in the future and so on.

31
00:02:58,477 --> 00:03:09,662
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, when you start uprooting that, it's not going to happen overnight and it's not just like a flipping a switch and now everything is suddenly not self and not mayātā and so on.

32
00:03:11,522 --> 00:03:19,566
[SPEAKER_03]: The coarser symptoms of that ownership that you have in regard to the aggregates, the coarser symptoms would

33
00:03:20,796 --> 00:03:21,237
[SPEAKER_03]: You fall off.

34
00:03:21,837 --> 00:03:27,523
[SPEAKER_03]: But, you're still gonna have subtler levels of identification and ownership.

35
00:03:27,744 --> 00:03:28,144
[SPEAKER_03]: With what?

36
00:03:28,264 --> 00:03:33,030
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, with form, or with feeling, or with perception, or with activities, or with consciousness.

37
00:03:33,430 --> 00:03:35,132
[SPEAKER_03]: Can't be something other than that.

38
00:03:37,294 --> 00:03:42,080
[SPEAKER_03]: For him, he's already... Well, he already, yeah, he already has had some understanding.

39
00:03:42,848 --> 00:03:49,053
[SPEAKER_03]: Because that question that he asked already implies knowledge to a degree of distinguishing between, oh, what is my self?

40
00:03:49,173 --> 00:03:49,654
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, right.

41
00:03:49,674 --> 00:03:54,678
[SPEAKER_03]: Because see, if he approached and said, okay, that atta and that anatta that the Lord teaches, what is that?

42
00:03:54,698 --> 00:03:55,959
[SPEAKER_03]: Then she says, oh, it's the five aggregates.

43
00:03:56,019 --> 00:03:57,340
[SPEAKER_03]: That would not be an accurate answer.

44
00:03:57,740 --> 00:03:59,001
[SPEAKER_03]: That would be quite a misleading answer.

45
00:03:59,622 --> 00:04:00,943
[SPEAKER_03]: Because that is not what atta is.

46
00:04:01,733 --> 00:04:02,894
[SPEAKER_03]: Atta is the result of that.

47
00:04:03,114 --> 00:04:04,275
[SPEAKER_03]: But what is that?

48
00:04:04,315 --> 00:04:05,316
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, it's that ownership.

49
00:04:05,716 --> 00:04:08,018
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's what he was referring to when he asked about sakāyā.

50
00:04:08,858 --> 00:04:16,264
[SPEAKER_03]: And, yeah, can you find any sense of ownership apart, outside, away from this body, this experience as a whole?

51
00:04:16,404 --> 00:04:16,524
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

52
00:04:17,525 --> 00:04:24,370
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's why it kind of implies that embodiment of ownership, it's bound with this, with this form, with this what you are.

53
00:04:25,360 --> 00:04:26,241
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, to what extent?

54
00:04:26,321 --> 00:04:34,406
[SPEAKER_03]: It's to the extent you've been maintaining the ownership carelessly, but unless a person is an arahant, there's always going to be some degree of it present.

55
00:04:37,148 --> 00:04:39,410
[SPEAKER_03]: That conceit, identification and ownership.

56
00:04:41,020 --> 00:04:45,944
[SPEAKER_00]: It's also interesting that thus corresponds five, I assume, aggregates.

57
00:04:46,525 --> 00:04:52,790
[SPEAKER_00]: It's also the summary of the First Noble Truth of Dukkha.

58
00:04:52,970 --> 00:04:55,392
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the exact same, not anything different.

59
00:04:56,553 --> 00:04:57,094
[SPEAKER_03]: Right, right, right.

60
00:04:57,114 --> 00:05:01,117
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, yeah, well, it's... it's the same inside, really.

61
00:05:01,637 --> 00:05:02,578
[SPEAKER_03]: Just explained in...

62
00:05:02,598 --> 00:05:04,240
[SPEAKER_03]: In different ways, different terms.

63
00:05:04,280 --> 00:05:05,781
[SPEAKER_03]: Described differently, but it's the same.

64
00:05:06,802 --> 00:05:09,466
[SPEAKER_03]: Because the... the root... the ownership is the root of suffering.

65
00:05:09,706 --> 00:05:12,510
[SPEAKER_03]: The identification is the root of suffering.

66
00:05:12,530 --> 00:05:13,932
[SPEAKER_03]: The craving is the root of suffering.

67
00:05:15,134 --> 00:05:16,716
[SPEAKER_03]: So, when there is craving, there is ownership.

68
00:05:16,736 --> 00:05:17,978
[SPEAKER_02]: And there's ownership as craving.

69
00:05:19,883 --> 00:05:25,326
[SPEAKER_02]: But the identification is not explicit, like, I am this, or I am that feeling, or I am this body, or I am...

70
00:05:25,386 --> 00:05:25,706
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

71
00:05:26,127 --> 00:05:26,367
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not.

72
00:05:26,387 --> 00:05:29,028
[SPEAKER_03]: It's more... That's already like...

73
00:05:29,048 --> 00:05:31,209
[SPEAKER_03]: Implied in your... how you act, basically.

74
00:05:31,229 --> 00:05:32,190
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, how you perceive.

75
00:05:32,510 --> 00:05:34,051
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, that's also...

76
00:05:34,211 --> 00:05:35,152
[SPEAKER_03]: It presupposes.

77
00:05:35,832 --> 00:05:39,114
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, any perception you have, it presupposes already ownership.

78
00:05:39,414 --> 00:05:41,815
[SPEAKER_03]: It's already there, as an underlying basis.

79
00:05:41,916 --> 00:05:42,156
[SPEAKER_03]: So...

80
00:05:42,976 --> 00:05:43,316
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

81
00:05:43,336 --> 00:05:46,938
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not on the level of choice whereby I choose to not identify.

82
00:05:46,978 --> 00:05:49,339
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, this aggregate is impermanent and this is impermanent.

83
00:05:49,359 --> 00:05:51,720
[SPEAKER_03]: You're doing that with your ownership already.

84
00:05:52,460 --> 00:05:53,801
[SPEAKER_04]: It's non-specific.

85
00:05:54,721 --> 00:05:54,901
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

86
00:05:54,941 --> 00:06:01,044
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the only way to kind of remove the ownership is to start undermining it indirectly.

87
00:06:01,696 --> 00:06:06,837
[SPEAKER_03]: Because whatever you directly take to tell yourself that it's not yours, well, that experience as a whole is yours.

88
00:06:07,398 --> 00:06:12,979
[SPEAKER_03]: So, then you... that's what we said, like with... with atta, he would be perceiving an atta.

89
00:06:13,099 --> 00:06:14,479
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, this is not self, this is not that.

90
00:06:14,519 --> 00:06:17,460
[SPEAKER_03]: But perceiving the not-self is done with your sense of self.

91
00:06:18,861 --> 00:06:23,402
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's not... it's not just so easily... you can't so easily wiggle out of it.

92
00:06:25,185 --> 00:06:29,226
[SPEAKER_05]: And the second question is, what is the source of sakāya, that identity?

93
00:06:29,447 --> 00:06:30,727
[SPEAKER_05]: And you basically answer that.

94
00:06:31,207 --> 00:06:36,849
[SPEAKER_05]: It is the craving for the perpetuation of being, or existence, and delighting and lusting here and there.

95
00:06:38,470 --> 00:06:51,374
[SPEAKER_03]: That's what keeps your ownership perpetuated, which is what then simultaneously feeds this gratuitous assumption of I am, and who I am, and what I am, and to what extent I am, and what is mine, and so on.

96
00:06:52,855 --> 00:06:53,355
[SPEAKER_03]: It's a symptom.

97
00:06:53,595 --> 00:06:53,975
[SPEAKER_03]: It's a symptom.

98
00:06:55,115 --> 00:06:56,855
[SPEAKER_05]: So, what is the ending of sakāyā?

99
00:06:57,315 --> 00:07:08,298
[SPEAKER_03]: Removal of the craving, desire, restraining desire and lust, till the craving is understood, abandoned, dispassion, experienced, there is no more ownership, then thus sakāyā cannot possibly persist.

100
00:07:09,338 --> 00:07:15,279
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, the complete dispassion, abandoning, rejection, freedom and attachment from craving.

101
00:07:16,860 --> 00:07:22,181
[SPEAKER_04]: But that's an indirect process in terms of allowing craving

102
00:07:23,650 --> 00:07:32,237
[SPEAKER_04]: To arise without acting out of it is... you're not trying to get rid of the craving, in terms of not allowing it to arise.

103
00:07:32,497 --> 00:07:33,838
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, that's a kind of a topic in itself.

104
00:07:33,938 --> 00:07:35,099
[SPEAKER_03]: It's unavoidable.

105
00:07:35,600 --> 00:07:37,782
[SPEAKER_03]: You have to get rid of the craving.

106
00:07:38,022 --> 00:07:39,543
[SPEAKER_03]: You have to abandon the craving.

107
00:07:40,144 --> 00:07:42,766
[SPEAKER_03]: But what does that getting rid of mean and abandoning?

108
00:07:42,786 --> 00:07:44,087
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, we covered that in other talks.

109
00:07:44,744 --> 00:07:46,004
[SPEAKER_03]: It does not mean denying it.

110
00:07:46,264 --> 00:07:52,086
[SPEAKER_03]: It does not mean, get rid of this, get rid of this, because that's just another thing you're doing out of your craving now, against that.

111
00:07:52,426 --> 00:07:53,726
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you're full of aversion towards it.

112
00:07:53,746 --> 00:07:54,366
[SPEAKER_03]: Satisfying it.

113
00:07:55,146 --> 00:07:55,726
[SPEAKER_03]: Certainly not.

114
00:07:55,946 --> 00:08:03,548
[SPEAKER_03]: So, basically, whether you're satisfying it, or you're trying to deny it, or you're ignoring it, all of those would be acting out of that craving.

115
00:08:03,768 --> 00:08:08,229
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's exactly why the practice of dhamma is subtle, and it's not just a question of countering.

116
00:08:08,249 --> 00:08:11,730
[SPEAKER_03]: When this arises with that arises, it's about discerning the middle way in between.

117
00:08:12,390 --> 00:08:12,610
[SPEAKER_03]: But,

118
00:08:13,711 --> 00:08:16,814
[SPEAKER_03]: As far as the answer to the question goes, yes, by abandonment or craving.

119
00:08:16,994 --> 00:08:20,117
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, the next question can be asked, what, how do I abandon that?

120
00:08:20,517 --> 00:08:26,282
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, then you go to every talk we did on the gradual training, the entirety of it, that's how you abandon it.

121
00:08:28,104 --> 00:08:31,427
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, so that is, yeah, how, and it's the Noble Eightfold Path.

122
00:08:32,228 --> 00:08:34,430
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's how you... Oh, well, there you go.

123
00:08:34,470 --> 00:08:35,511
[SPEAKER_03]: Maybe just read the sutra then.

124
00:08:37,856 --> 00:08:48,979
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, exactly, because there is exactly that tendency of, alright, same with you, like, okay, so this is not myself now, so I'm dealing with it, or, okay, so I'm not craving now, I shall abandon this craving.

125
00:08:49,039 --> 00:08:51,780
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, craving, get rid of the craving, I must not crave, I must not crave.

126
00:08:52,140 --> 00:08:58,861
[SPEAKER_03]: No, that's all just wishful thinking now, and you're trying to jump at the end, at the result of what, yes, you need to abandon it.

127
00:08:59,222 --> 00:09:04,883
[SPEAKER_03]: Same when we spoke about those hindrances before, when people can't come to terms with learning how to be

128
00:09:05,755 --> 00:09:22,060
[SPEAKER_03]: You're not being patient in regard to them, but feeling fully justified in being averse towards the mind of, you know, experiencing pleasure or pain and just trying to get rid of it, just trying to counter it and antidote it with a method, with a technique, with... and feeling justified in doing so.

129
00:09:23,101 --> 00:09:23,921
[SPEAKER_03]: As a... No, no, no.

130
00:09:24,281 --> 00:09:27,222
[SPEAKER_03]: You have to be able to see that mind for what it is.

131
00:09:27,915 --> 00:09:31,418
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you have to recognize that, well, you are already within the craving.

132
00:09:31,578 --> 00:09:33,059
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you can't just...

133
00:09:33,079 --> 00:09:36,882
[SPEAKER_03]: I got to get rid of this craving without that being also part of that craving.

134
00:09:37,683 --> 00:09:39,864
[SPEAKER_03]: So, then, how do I deal with this?

135
00:09:39,945 --> 00:09:40,905
[SPEAKER_03]: That's a valid question.

136
00:09:40,945 --> 00:09:50,253
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, you deal with this by not trying to get rid of it, by not thinking it's on the level of choice or antidoting your unpleasant experiences that you are fundamentally still averse to.

137
00:09:51,064 --> 00:10:04,414
[SPEAKER_03]: But by recognizing, right, that's the gradual training, the Noble Eightfold Path, the entirety, starting from the livelihood, speech, bodily actions, precepts, sense restraint, all of that is your way out.

138
00:10:04,594 --> 00:10:11,139
[SPEAKER_03]: Not like something you do on a side, you know, sometimes, but then I relax on the weekends, but then I'm back onto it on Monday.

139
00:10:11,480 --> 00:10:11,800
[SPEAKER_03]: No, no, no.

140
00:10:11,880 --> 00:10:14,462
[SPEAKER_03]: Your whole existence is being reshaped by that

141
00:10:14,842 --> 00:10:16,623
[SPEAKER_03]: Entirety of the Noble Eightfold Path.

142
00:10:16,643 --> 00:10:20,084
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why, when the Buddha did describe what's the way out, that's the way out.

143
00:10:20,164 --> 00:10:22,746
[SPEAKER_03]: There is nothing shorter than that, that can be the way out.

144
00:10:23,386 --> 00:10:24,907
[SPEAKER_03]: If it were, it's not the way out.

145
00:10:26,327 --> 00:10:30,589
[SPEAKER_02]: What comes up often is, I want something palpable to do.

146
00:10:31,690 --> 00:10:32,770
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how to deal with that?

147
00:10:33,470 --> 00:10:34,771
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, that's too ambitious for me.

148
00:10:34,791 --> 00:10:35,751
[SPEAKER_03]: There's a lot of palpable stuff.

149
00:10:36,111 --> 00:10:37,152
[SPEAKER_03]: Such as keeping the precepts?

150
00:10:38,297 --> 00:10:39,318
[SPEAKER_03]: That's very palpable.

151
00:10:40,018 --> 00:10:42,819
[SPEAKER_03]: When you want to look at things that are rooted in greed, aversion, delusion, you don't.

152
00:10:43,119 --> 00:10:44,360
[SPEAKER_03]: That's something you can do as well.

153
00:10:45,060 --> 00:10:46,881
[SPEAKER_03]: Keep your mouth shut when you want to say something.

154
00:10:47,001 --> 00:10:48,001
[SPEAKER_03]: That's something you can do.

155
00:10:48,962 --> 00:10:50,682
[SPEAKER_03]: Don't go out into the crowd or company.

156
00:10:50,742 --> 00:10:51,463
[SPEAKER_03]: Stay alone in the group.

157
00:10:51,483 --> 00:10:52,463
[SPEAKER_03]: That's something you can do.

158
00:10:53,123 --> 00:10:55,885
[SPEAKER_03]: But see, but it's not what I want to do.

159
00:10:55,905 --> 00:11:00,727
[SPEAKER_03]: So, give me something palpable to do that I also want to do and that will result in the immediate pleasure.

160
00:11:01,507 --> 00:11:03,708
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, that's not the practice of the dhamma then.

161
00:11:05,348 --> 00:11:10,511
[SPEAKER_03]: But, yeah, depending how you look at it, there are lots of palpable things to do, and more than enough, actually.

162
00:11:10,811 --> 00:11:11,512
[SPEAKER_05]: Give me something quick.

163
00:11:11,832 --> 00:11:14,253
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, well, that's really what's being asked.

164
00:11:14,293 --> 00:11:15,674
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

165
00:11:15,694 --> 00:11:20,497
[SPEAKER_03]: Right, but come on, just give me something that would take me all the way there, without all of this.

166
00:11:21,857 --> 00:11:24,459
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, to find something I can hammer away at.

167
00:11:24,519 --> 00:11:26,100
[SPEAKER_05]: Just straightforward.

168
00:11:26,120 --> 00:11:33,564
[SPEAKER_03]: Just give me a view that I can agree with, that will make me feel like I'm absolved from responsibility and duty, and thus I'll be saved.

169
00:11:33,784 --> 00:11:34,785
[SPEAKER_03]: Just, that's it.

170
00:11:40,083 --> 00:11:56,797
[SPEAKER_05]: So, is the assumption of the upādāna, in terms of the five aggregates, is the assumption of ownership the exact same thing as the five assumed aggregates, or is this assumption one thing and the five assumed aggregates another?

171
00:11:57,318 --> 00:12:05,245
[SPEAKER_05]: So, is my identity separate from those aggregates, or is it the same?

172
00:12:06,610 --> 00:12:13,392
[SPEAKER_04]: The five aggregates don't depend on craving at all, or clinging at all, but clinging depends on the aggregates.

173
00:12:13,492 --> 00:12:18,713
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's like a parasite, almost, in... What is a parasite?

174
00:12:20,213 --> 00:12:21,054
[SPEAKER_04]: The assumption.

175
00:12:21,154 --> 00:12:21,814
[SPEAKER_04]: The assumption.

176
00:12:21,834 --> 00:12:22,214
[SPEAKER_04]: Upādāna.

177
00:12:22,234 --> 00:12:22,794
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, yeah.

178
00:12:23,454 --> 00:12:29,816
[SPEAKER_03]: So, upādāna is not the form, feeling, perception, saṅkhara's consciousness.

179
00:12:29,836 --> 00:12:30,856
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, it depends on...

180
00:12:31,036 --> 00:12:31,936
[SPEAKER_02]: But it depends on them.

181
00:12:32,456 --> 00:12:34,897
[SPEAKER_04]: No, the the five aggregates

182
00:12:36,627 --> 00:12:38,648
[SPEAKER_04]: Don't depend on the upādāna.

183
00:12:38,848 --> 00:12:40,608
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, but that's not the question.

184
00:12:40,948 --> 00:12:45,830
[SPEAKER_03]: The question is, is upādāna different from the five aggregates or not?

185
00:12:48,430 --> 00:12:50,331
[SPEAKER_04]: No, it's not separate from the five aggregates.

186
00:12:50,511 --> 00:12:51,491
