582
00:35:54,705 --> 00:36:09,836
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, well, yeah, I'm not saying that it's not also a thought, but it's like, you can have all sorts of particular thoughts about this, that and the other, but then, as a determination for speech, there would be something which is a bit... which is still a thought, but it seems to be more deeply rooted.

583
00:36:10,377 --> 00:36:10,797
[SPEAKER_05]: It assumes...

584
00:36:10,817 --> 00:36:11,758
[SPEAKER_03]: Deeply rooted into what?

585
00:36:16,321 --> 00:36:19,864
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, in relation to perhaps more particular thoughts.

586
00:36:20,893 --> 00:36:22,053
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's another thought, then?

587
00:36:22,313 --> 00:36:22,594
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, yeah.

588
00:36:22,614 --> 00:36:24,474
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm not denying that it is also another thought.

589
00:36:24,934 --> 00:36:27,615
[SPEAKER_03]: But what else is, if it's not just a thought?

590
00:36:27,935 --> 00:36:29,136
[SPEAKER_03]: Because you keep saying it's not...

591
00:36:29,256 --> 00:36:31,196
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm not denying it's also not a thought.

592
00:36:31,797 --> 00:36:34,777
[SPEAKER_03]: What else is it, beside the thought?

593
00:36:35,798 --> 00:36:36,738
[SPEAKER_03]: Can it be something else?

594
00:36:39,519 --> 00:36:39,699
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

595
00:36:39,799 --> 00:36:48,062
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe what I try to say is that... that it's... that it's held more deeply than sort of...

596
00:36:48,142 --> 00:36:48,702
[SPEAKER_03]: Deep to what?

597
00:36:50,018 --> 00:36:52,080
[SPEAKER_03]: What is the surface that you measure the depth from?

598
00:36:59,885 --> 00:37:01,086
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, let me put it the other way around.

599
00:37:03,128 --> 00:37:05,509
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you saying it's exactly on the same level as every other thought?

600
00:37:05,529 --> 00:37:10,493
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't want to give you the answer before you answer me.

601
00:37:13,936 --> 00:37:18,179
[SPEAKER_03]: We'll arrive at it, but it is quite useful where you...

602
00:37:18,259 --> 00:37:19,740
[SPEAKER_02]: It's quite useful where you started.

603
00:37:20,463 --> 00:37:24,206
[SPEAKER_03]: Because that's exactly why people don't understand the extent of thinking and pondering.

604
00:37:24,286 --> 00:37:25,787
[SPEAKER_03]: Because they keep thinking there's something more to it.

605
00:37:27,829 --> 00:37:32,273
[SPEAKER_03]: But that more, it's only more because you assumed it to be outside of your thinking and pondering.

606
00:37:33,053 --> 00:37:33,534
[SPEAKER_03]: And exactly.

607
00:37:33,754 --> 00:37:34,134
[SPEAKER_03]: Deeper.

608
00:37:34,735 --> 00:37:37,997
[SPEAKER_03]: But if you start looking at the depth, you realize, well, it can only be a thought.

609
00:37:38,838 --> 00:37:42,121
[SPEAKER_03]: So, for as long as I don't open my mouth and utter,

610
00:37:43,338 --> 00:37:45,119
[SPEAKER_03]: Voice, air, sounds...

611
00:37:46,200 --> 00:37:47,701
[SPEAKER_03]: I am not speaking.

612
00:37:47,941 --> 00:37:52,163
[SPEAKER_03]: It doesn't matter how much deeply I go into verbal thinking and pondering.

613
00:37:55,545 --> 00:37:59,007
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you want to stop the vāci-sankhāra?

614
00:37:59,427 --> 00:38:03,070
[SPEAKER_03]: Stop speaking, and stop assuming the speech through your thoughts.

615
00:38:03,250 --> 00:38:03,590
[SPEAKER_03]: That's it.

616
00:38:05,631 --> 00:38:12,355
[SPEAKER_03]: You don't have to shut your thoughts, or even verbal speaking, you don't have to shut down, in order to be quiet.

617
00:38:14,069 --> 00:38:15,829
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's not what a speech is.

618
00:38:16,970 --> 00:38:25,951
[SPEAKER_03]: It says, like, the vāci-sankhāra, the activity of breaking into speech, the necessary activity for it, is thinking and pondering.

619
00:38:26,011 --> 00:38:26,212
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

620
00:38:26,332 --> 00:38:26,852
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

621
00:38:26,872 --> 00:38:27,912
[SPEAKER_03]: On that verbal kind.

622
00:38:27,952 --> 00:38:34,493
[SPEAKER_03]: But you realize, for as long as you're not breaking into speech, it's gonna be thinking and pondering.

623
00:38:35,974 --> 00:38:42,995
[SPEAKER_03]: Doesn't matter how you feel in regard to it, and what assumptions float around, and if your mouth is all pressured to say something,

624
00:38:44,352 --> 00:38:46,833
[SPEAKER_03]: Breaking into speech happens in its own domain.

625
00:38:48,213 --> 00:38:49,774
[SPEAKER_03]: And this does not enter that domain.

626
00:38:55,096 --> 00:38:59,257
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, what determines the breaking into speech, if it's not that thinking and pondering?

627
00:39:00,178 --> 00:39:04,119
[SPEAKER_03]: The domain of verbal possibility of speaking.

628
00:39:07,360 --> 00:39:09,581
[SPEAKER_03]: The domain of having a mouth that can utter voices.

629
00:39:12,699 --> 00:39:16,042
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but whether it does or not, still involves some sort of intention there.

630
00:39:16,863 --> 00:39:17,604
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, that's my point.

631
00:39:17,844 --> 00:39:20,327
[SPEAKER_03]: There is no transition of that intention.

632
00:39:20,447 --> 00:39:26,273
[SPEAKER_03]: It involves intention to think and ponder and say something, which is not on the level of speech.

633
00:39:27,534 --> 00:39:29,776
[SPEAKER_03]: When it is, it's because it came on from its own domain.

634
00:39:33,500 --> 00:39:34,922
[SPEAKER_03]: The speech comes from its own domain.

635
00:39:35,612 --> 00:39:38,634
[SPEAKER_03]: Because thinking and pondering to speak is in its own domain.

636
00:39:39,595 --> 00:39:40,416
[SPEAKER_03]: There is no overlap.

637
00:39:40,676 --> 00:39:43,879
[SPEAKER_03]: There is no one goes into the other and then your voice comes out.

638
00:39:45,220 --> 00:39:46,801
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the self-centered point of view.

639
00:39:47,361 --> 00:39:47,562
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

640
00:39:48,482 --> 00:39:54,667
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if you understand the extent of vittaka vichara correctly...

641
00:39:54,687 --> 00:39:56,309
[SPEAKER_03]: It's almost understanding the limit correctly.

642
00:39:57,033 --> 00:39:59,374
[SPEAKER_03]: That's what extent means, exactly, yeah.

643
00:39:59,534 --> 00:40:00,855
[SPEAKER_03]: And not assume that there is...

644
00:40:00,895 --> 00:40:04,636
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, this is the limit and then my speech is outside, which is another vittāka-vicāra.

645
00:40:05,376 --> 00:40:10,919
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you realize, well, how do then I access the domain of speech?

646
00:40:12,740 --> 00:40:13,580
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, by speaking.

647
00:40:15,321 --> 00:40:15,641
[SPEAKER_03]: That's it.

648
00:40:16,021 --> 00:40:17,121
[SPEAKER_03]: You can't access it with your thoughts.

649
00:40:17,341 --> 00:40:21,423
[SPEAKER_03]: In the same sense, your mind does not sit in your body, does not access your form.

650
00:40:21,843 --> 00:40:25,585
[SPEAKER_03]: It just overlaps through assumption and assumes it's in it and vice versa.

651
00:40:26,935 --> 00:40:30,856
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's that converging point of speech, of verbal saṅkhara.

652
00:40:31,956 --> 00:40:41,038
[SPEAKER_03]: Mouth is physical, sounds are physical, it's own level of the form, but it's your thinking and pondering that does not know its own extent, that you assume.

653
00:40:41,798 --> 00:40:44,339
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, I have said this, and it went out.

654
00:40:44,439 --> 00:40:44,579
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

655
00:40:45,319 --> 00:40:48,500
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm thinking of saying it, and then the body is speaking for me.

656
00:40:50,470 --> 00:40:51,431
[SPEAKER_01]: That's pretty much what I'm saying.

657
00:40:52,311 --> 00:41:00,896
[SPEAKER_03]: But now you can accept that as information, sure, but you really need to see it on the level of your acute thinking and pondering as a verbal saṅkhāra.

658
00:41:03,918 --> 00:41:10,002
[SPEAKER_01]: As a verbal saṅkhāra means that thinking and pondering determines the possibility... Means talking to yourself in your own mind.

659
00:41:11,943 --> 00:41:12,343
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, yes.

660
00:41:12,423 --> 00:41:13,344
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what it means practically.

661
00:41:13,624 --> 00:41:13,824
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

662
00:41:13,864 --> 00:41:14,865
[SPEAKER_03]: That's a verbal saṅkhāra.

663
00:41:15,565 --> 00:41:16,285
[SPEAKER_03]: But it's not speech.

664
00:41:16,586 --> 00:41:17,306
[SPEAKER_03]: But it's not speech.

665
00:41:17,626 --> 00:41:18,167
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not speech.

666
00:41:18,687 --> 00:41:18,787
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

667
00:41:19,670 --> 00:41:25,071
[SPEAKER_03]: It determines speech, but it's like... Well, see, why does it determine speech?

668
00:41:25,211 --> 00:41:26,892
[SPEAKER_03]: Let's... let me stop answering it.

669
00:41:26,992 --> 00:41:28,132
[SPEAKER_03]: Let me start asking questions.

670
00:41:29,092 --> 00:41:35,434
[SPEAKER_03]: Why does speaking to yourself, in your own mind, why is that a determination for breaking into speech?

671
00:41:37,094 --> 00:41:39,815
[SPEAKER_03]: Can you break into speech without having spoken?

672
00:41:40,535 --> 00:41:41,276
[SPEAKER_03]: No, impossible.

673
00:41:41,856 --> 00:41:42,316
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why.

674
00:41:42,856 --> 00:41:44,236
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, why is that?

675
00:41:44,296 --> 00:41:44,656
[SPEAKER_03]: Why?

676
00:41:44,916 --> 00:41:46,757
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, that's an irrelevant question.

677
00:41:47,977 --> 00:41:50,279
[SPEAKER_03]: There is no... oh, well, this is how that happens.

678
00:41:51,019 --> 00:41:55,163
[SPEAKER_03]: What is inseparable is that there is no breath apart from the body.

679
00:41:55,543 --> 00:41:57,705
[SPEAKER_03]: Just... that is the base saṅkhara.

680
00:41:58,465 --> 00:42:02,989
[SPEAKER_03]: What is inseparable is there is no breaking into speech without verbalized thinking.

681
00:42:04,870 --> 00:42:07,092
[SPEAKER_03]: That is just the necessary basis for it.

682
00:42:07,772 --> 00:42:12,136
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, consequently, for the third saṅkhara, the mind and perception and feeling.

683
00:42:12,396 --> 00:42:13,557
[SPEAKER_03]: It's inseparable from it.

684
00:42:14,547 --> 00:42:24,170
[SPEAKER_03]: And the reason why I'm saying this is because speech in particular, obviously, it's quite relevant for when comes people to practice meditation and so on, whatever their meditation means for them.

685
00:42:24,970 --> 00:42:32,132
[SPEAKER_03]: But the point is, no matter how differently you're going about it, pretty much what is common is that everybody overlooks it.

686
00:42:33,613 --> 00:42:39,814
[SPEAKER_03]: Everybody just wants to shut down the thoughts and the mental speaking, confusing it for this is a speech, this is a chatter.

687
00:42:40,635 --> 00:42:41,455
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, actually it isn't.

688
00:42:42,749 --> 00:42:44,530
[SPEAKER_03]: It's unrestrained thinking and pondering.

689
00:42:44,550 --> 00:42:45,251
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, that's for sure.

690
00:42:46,452 --> 00:42:57,099
[SPEAKER_03]: But one thing is to restrain it, harness it, hone it, and another thing is to try to get rid of it and get that peace of no thinking.

691
00:42:57,559 --> 00:43:05,384
[SPEAKER_03]: So first, develop it rightly, see the distinction, the different extent, the necessary basis, and then you'll know how to count down the rest.

692
00:43:07,686 --> 00:43:11,849
[SPEAKER_03]: And those are the actual reflections that even Bodhisattva was doing, what we mentioned in that sutta.

693
00:43:12,388 --> 00:43:16,952
[SPEAKER_03]: He says, oh, he would have a, I don't know, a hindrance of lust arise or thoughts of cruelty.

694
00:43:17,772 --> 00:43:19,594
[SPEAKER_03]: He wasn't trying to get rid of it or stop thinking it.

695
00:43:19,654 --> 00:43:19,834
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

696
00:43:19,994 --> 00:43:25,298
[SPEAKER_03]: He would start telling to himself, internally, this is for my own harm.

697
00:43:26,059 --> 00:43:28,140
[SPEAKER_03]: Is this for benefit of me or others?

698
00:43:28,721 --> 00:43:30,222
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

699
00:43:30,402 --> 00:43:33,784
[SPEAKER_03]: What has this resulted in the past, engaging with sensuality?

700
00:43:33,824 --> 00:43:39,649
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, resulted in increasing the the the kind of unpredictability of my own mind,

701
00:43:41,464 --> 00:43:45,385
[SPEAKER_03]: The lack of composure, the bad choices out of non-perspective.

702
00:43:45,945 --> 00:43:52,047
[SPEAKER_03]: You can tell all those things, not mechanically, but in relation to an enduring pressure of lust.

703
00:43:52,887 --> 00:43:54,648
[SPEAKER_03]: Same, you have pressure of ill-will.

704
00:43:54,708 --> 00:43:56,208
[SPEAKER_03]: Sure, you have a choice to act.

705
00:43:56,348 --> 00:43:57,408
[SPEAKER_03]: Let's not act for a second.

706
00:43:58,469 --> 00:44:04,950
[SPEAKER_03]: Stop, sit down quietly and review it, as the Buddha would say, in your own mind.

707
00:44:04,970 --> 00:44:10,352
[SPEAKER_03]: Wait, this state that I'm being pressured by now, how many times it happened so far?

708
00:44:11,235 --> 00:44:14,637
[SPEAKER_03]: By the way, I'm speaking this so you can hear me, but you should do this in your own head.

709
00:44:16,277 --> 00:44:17,458
[SPEAKER_03]: How many times I've done this?

710
00:44:18,358 --> 00:44:18,699
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't know.

711
00:44:19,519 --> 00:44:23,001
[SPEAKER_03]: Did it ever result in completely free me from anger?

712
00:44:23,201 --> 00:44:25,422
[SPEAKER_03]: Or did it ever not make things worse?

713
00:44:25,622 --> 00:44:27,363
[SPEAKER_03]: Or did it not ever make me remorseful after?

714
00:44:27,403 --> 00:44:27,583
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

715
00:44:27,783 --> 00:44:30,404
[SPEAKER_03]: So it's it's all it's always universal and wholesome.

716
00:44:31,025 --> 00:44:31,305
[SPEAKER_03]: That's it.

717
00:44:31,325 --> 00:44:33,306
[SPEAKER_03]: You don't even need to answer now whether you should act or not.

718
00:44:34,126 --> 00:44:38,868
[SPEAKER_03]: You would have already subsided that anger by maintaining the correct perspective in regard to it.

719
00:44:39,809 --> 00:44:40,049
[SPEAKER_03]: Now,

720
00:44:41,120 --> 00:44:43,101
[SPEAKER_03]: Do that thoroughly, day in, day out.

721
00:44:45,001 --> 00:44:47,742
[SPEAKER_03]: Whenever anything unwholesome comes, you review it.

722
00:44:49,242 --> 00:44:53,623
[SPEAKER_03]: Ask yourself direct questions in regard to the recent experience, not mechanical questions.

723
00:44:54,504 --> 00:45:02,486
[SPEAKER_03]: Or just go through the motions of these mantra, repeating mantra questions, to get that experience ahead, when I actually did gain the perspective rightly.

724
00:45:02,606 --> 00:45:04,206
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh no, it's the right perspective, right.

725
00:45:05,407 --> 00:45:06,627
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you go back to it,

726
00:45:08,548 --> 00:45:12,232
[SPEAKER_03]: Each time things arise, you question them rightly, verbally, internally.

727
00:45:13,953 --> 00:45:18,478
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, yeah, the time will come when you realize, okay, there's nothing unwholesome pressuring me.

728
00:45:19,058 --> 00:45:24,644
[SPEAKER_03]: Why don't I stop this questioning and reviewing and constant maintenance of these wholesome thoughts?

729
00:45:25,424 --> 00:45:32,271
[SPEAKER_03]: Let me just be mindful of this non-speaking mouth and stop talking even verbally in my mind.

730
00:45:33,702 --> 00:45:35,622
[SPEAKER_03]: And you would have sufficiently tamed your mind by then.

731
00:45:35,862 --> 00:45:42,844
[SPEAKER_03]: And by then, you could even plan for the second jhāna, which is exactly what Bodhisattva did.

732
00:45:46,204 --> 00:45:59,727
[SPEAKER_03]: But, by not seeing where the line is, you'll be trying to get rid of the thoughts, or ignoring the verbal thinking, thinking, oh, that's just... this is where the practice is, while the actual root of the context and perspective starts with

733
00:46:00,717 --> 00:46:02,661
[SPEAKER_03]: Verbalizing what is right here present.

734
00:46:03,182 --> 00:46:12,820
[SPEAKER_03]: Not mechanically, like they did in Mahāsī, but actually with a question that points at the perspective of understanding what's presently endured.

735
00:46:15,978 --> 00:46:21,079
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's developing perspective, developing strength, that's developing samādhi.

736
00:46:21,119 --> 00:46:25,740
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the step that's kind of overlooked, because of which you won't be able to go very far.

737
00:46:26,280 --> 00:46:36,321
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why even in the suttas, when somebody wanted, like Maha Moggallāna wanted to understand the first jhāna, when the Buddha encouraged him, he was, he told, he said to himself, basically, first jhāna, first jhāna, what is first jhāna?

738
00:46:36,802 --> 00:46:44,803
[SPEAKER_03]: And started describing the factors, not on the level of information, but on the level of attending what is there, and being simultaneously described.

739
00:46:45,742 --> 00:46:46,802
[SPEAKER_03]: By thinking and pondering.

740
00:46:48,543 --> 00:46:49,843
[SPEAKER_03]: Without breaking into speech.

741
00:46:52,563 --> 00:46:56,244
[SPEAKER_04]: But it all comes from this first chariot of not acting out of it.

742
00:46:56,384 --> 00:46:57,024
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

743
00:46:57,064 --> 00:47:02,526
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, see, if you don't put that first, all what I'm saying is, you could even get a glimpse of it.

744
00:47:02,686 --> 00:47:04,106
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, I can maintain the perspective.

745
00:47:04,526 --> 00:47:12,708
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, out of lack of the proper development, you're gonna re-infuse it with the same old attitude of wanting a shortcut.

746
00:47:13,328 --> 00:47:31,124
[SPEAKER_03]: So, instead of then enduring what's present, questioning yourself rightly to reveal the motivation in the context, you'll be just mechanically verbalizing, as I said, mantras, or like, oh, I'm sitting, I'm sitting, I'm touching, I'm feeling, I'm feeling, I'm perceiving, I'm perceiving, completely ignoring your intention behind it.

747
00:47:31,725 --> 00:47:38,151
[SPEAKER_03]: So, yeah, verbalize whatever you want, and then verbalize the question, why are you verbalizing that in the first place?

748
00:47:38,311 --> 00:47:39,512
[SPEAKER_03]: Why is this relevant to me?

749
00:47:40,785 --> 00:47:43,226
[SPEAKER_03]: Why am I trying to verbalize these things so desperately?

750
00:47:43,706 --> 00:47:44,506
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, because I don't want to suffer.

751
00:47:44,546 --> 00:47:44,906
[SPEAKER_03]: All right.

752
00:47:44,926 --> 00:47:46,207
[SPEAKER_03]: So, suffering is back here.

753
00:47:46,607 --> 00:47:47,747
[SPEAKER_03]: And how am I dealing with it?

754
00:47:48,668 --> 00:47:57,431
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, by verbalizing random methods that I made mechanical, because I'm lacking proper development of sense restraint, gradual training.

755
00:47:58,011 --> 00:48:01,012
[SPEAKER_03]: I have not endured things rightly on the right level sufficiently enough.

756
00:48:01,512 --> 00:48:02,292
[SPEAKER_03]: So, I just can't help it.

757
00:48:02,532 --> 00:48:04,773
[SPEAKER_03]: I just need a fix, a shortcut.

758
00:48:06,047 --> 00:48:12,732
[SPEAKER_03]: So then exactly, even if the Buddha himself is just telling you exactly what to do, you will make that into a method of an impersonal kind.

759
00:48:21,059 --> 00:48:30,747
[SPEAKER_05]: So just once again, what is the physical activator, the verbal activator and the mental, the citta activator?

760
00:48:32,027 --> 00:48:34,528
[SPEAKER_05]: Breathing is the physical saṃkhāra.

761
00:48:35,508 --> 00:48:37,609
[SPEAKER_05]: Thinking and pondering is the verbal saṃkhāra.

762
00:48:37,729 --> 00:48:40,830
[SPEAKER_05]: Perception and feeling are the mental saṃkhāra.

763
00:48:41,731 --> 00:48:44,712
[SPEAKER_05]: How is breathing a physical saṃkhāra?

764
00:48:45,152 --> 00:48:47,573
[SPEAKER_05]: How is thinking and pondering a verbal saṃkhāra?

765
00:48:47,613 --> 00:48:50,754
[SPEAKER_05]: How is perception and feeling a mental saṃkhāra?

766
00:48:51,748 --> 00:48:52,908
[SPEAKER_05]: Breathing is physical.

767
00:48:53,449 --> 00:48:55,510
[SPEAKER_05]: It's tied up with the body.

768
00:48:55,530 --> 00:48:58,271
[SPEAKER_05]: That is why breathing is a physical saṃkhāra.

769
00:48:58,791 --> 00:49:03,113
[SPEAKER_05]: Having thought and pondered, reflected, you break into speech.

770
00:49:04,213 --> 00:49:07,555
[SPEAKER_05]: That's why thought and reflection are verbal saṃkhāra.

771
00:49:07,815 --> 00:49:09,295
[SPEAKER_05]: Perception and feeling are mental.

772
00:49:09,956 --> 00:49:11,176
[SPEAKER_05]: They are tied up with the mind.

773
00:49:11,436 --> 00:49:15,498
[SPEAKER_05]: That's why perception and feeling are mental processes, eh, activators.

774
00:49:17,634 --> 00:49:21,777
[SPEAKER_05]: So, how does someone attain the ending of perception and feeling?

775
00:49:22,818 --> 00:49:25,460
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, the complete cessation of saṃkhāras.

776
00:49:30,084 --> 00:49:31,806
[SPEAKER_05]: The complete cessation of saṃkhāras.

777
00:49:31,906 --> 00:49:35,048
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, that's the most refined one.

778
00:49:35,268 --> 00:49:40,933
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if you can attain that, it means you would have abandoned the speech and the breathing, for that matter.

779
00:49:45,258 --> 00:49:48,721
[SPEAKER_03]: So... Well, by doing everything else we said, up to this point.

780
00:49:48,741 --> 00:49:53,705
[SPEAKER_00]: I find the answer to this one quite good in relation to many other things as well.

781
00:49:54,325 --> 00:49:54,785
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, yeah.

782
00:49:55,426 --> 00:50:02,011
[SPEAKER_05]: She says, a monk who is entering such an attainment does not think, I will enter the cessation of perception and feeling.

783
00:50:02,491 --> 00:50:02,731
[SPEAKER_05]: Why?

784
00:50:02,952 --> 00:50:05,033
[SPEAKER_01]: I am entering the cessation of perception and feeling.

785
00:50:05,534 --> 00:50:05,694
[SPEAKER_05]: Why?

786
00:50:05,714 --> 00:50:07,815
[SPEAKER_05]: I've entered the cessation of perception and feeling.

787
00:50:08,516 --> 00:50:12,399
[SPEAKER_05]: Rather, their mind has been previously developed so as to lead to such a state.

788
00:50:13,400 --> 00:50:13,740
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

789
00:50:14,120 --> 00:50:17,583
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's the result of developing the same principle way before that.

790
00:50:19,524 --> 00:50:21,546
[SPEAKER_05]: But there's there's no I am.

791
00:50:21,666 --> 00:50:23,387
[SPEAKER_03]: There's no room even for such a thought, yeah.

792
00:50:23,888 --> 00:50:25,529
[SPEAKER_03]: No, because that's a perception.

793
00:50:25,849 --> 00:50:30,753
[SPEAKER_05]: Oh, this must be perception, the cessation of perception feeling.

794
00:50:32,995 --> 00:50:39,820
[SPEAKER_05]: So, yes, which sees first for a monk who has, who is entering the cessation of perception feeling?

795
00:50:40,961 --> 00:50:43,523
[SPEAKER_05]: Physical, verbal or mental activators?

796
00:50:44,658 --> 00:50:44,978
[SPEAKER_03]: Which one?

797
00:50:47,080 --> 00:50:47,981
[SPEAKER_03]: Verbal.

798
00:50:48,782 --> 00:50:50,764
[SPEAKER_03]: Verbal ceases first.

799
00:50:50,784 --> 00:50:54,808
[SPEAKER_05]: Verbal ceases first, then the physical, then the mental.

800
00:50:57,770 --> 00:50:58,571
[SPEAKER_05]: So, then the breathing.

801
00:51:00,092 --> 00:51:00,293
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

802
00:51:00,833 --> 00:51:02,054
[SPEAKER_03]: It would cease before the mental.

803
00:51:06,518 --> 00:51:10,042
[SPEAKER_05]: So, how does someone emerge from cessation of perception and feeling?

804
00:51:11,864 --> 00:51:20,869
[SPEAKER_05]: A monk who is emerging from such an attainment does not think, I will emerge, I am emerging, or I have emerged from cessation of perception and feeling.

805
00:51:21,449 --> 00:51:25,952
[SPEAKER_05]: Rather, their mind has been previously developed so as to lead to such a state.

806
00:51:28,113 --> 00:51:32,395
[SPEAKER_05]: So, which arise first for a monk who is emerging from the cessation of perception and feeling?

807
00:51:34,036 --> 00:51:35,137
[SPEAKER_05]: Physical, verbal or mental?

808
00:51:36,940 --> 00:51:38,221
[SPEAKER_05]: And it's just the opposite, basically.

809
00:51:38,582 --> 00:51:42,645
[SPEAKER_05]: Mental arise first, then the physical, then the verbal.

810
00:51:48,050 --> 00:51:57,579
[SPEAKER_05]: So, when a monk has emerged from the attainment of this cessation of perception and feeling, how many kinds of pressure or contexts do they experience?

811
00:51:59,220 --> 00:52:02,963
[SPEAKER_05]: They experience three kinds of pressures or contexts.

812
00:52:05,145 --> 00:52:13,667
[SPEAKER_05]: Emptiness, sunyata, signlessness, nimitta, and undirected desire, apanīto.

813
00:52:13,867 --> 00:52:16,328
[SPEAKER_05]: It's like... Non-specific.

814
00:52:16,468 --> 00:52:22,149
[SPEAKER_05]: Non-specific desire, non-specific intention.

815
00:52:22,369 --> 00:52:26,130
[SPEAKER_05]: I can't even understand it.

816
00:52:26,950 --> 00:52:27,750
[SPEAKER_05]: I can't visualize it.

817
00:52:27,990 --> 00:52:29,711
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, it goes hand-in-hand with signlessness.

818
00:52:30,571 --> 00:52:32,031
[SPEAKER_01]: Sign is the specificity.

819
00:52:33,332 --> 00:52:34,912
[SPEAKER_03]: See, the mind is established upon non-

820
00:52:35,986 --> 00:52:49,412
[SPEAKER_03]: Non-specificity of signs, then, the only thing that can accompany is equally non-specific direction, which you wouldn't even call a desire, from the point of view of desires, that are always bound with the specific.

821
00:52:51,053 --> 00:52:52,133
[SPEAKER_03]: And the emptiness, exactly.

822
00:52:52,353 --> 00:52:53,434
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not the emptiness.

823
00:52:54,134 --> 00:53:02,198
[SPEAKER_03]: It's the empty of ownership, the empty of self, hollow of lust, aversion, hollow of that burning core that was existing before.

824
00:53:03,267 --> 00:53:05,128
[SPEAKER_05]: But it's still a contact.

825
00:53:05,528 --> 00:53:07,029
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, again, sure.

826
00:53:07,569 --> 00:53:20,774
[SPEAKER_03]: Technically, you can call it a contact, but only by abandoning the contact that non-Arahant has, you can understand the contact of an Arahant.

827
00:53:21,054 --> 00:53:22,575
[SPEAKER_03]: Because that's pretty much an Arahant.

828
00:53:22,655 --> 00:53:23,516
[SPEAKER_03]: That's an Arahant.

829
00:53:25,156 --> 00:53:26,897
[SPEAKER_03]: Maybe the same as you left, I suppose, but...

830
00:53:29,875 --> 00:53:36,018
[SPEAKER_05]: So, when a monk has emerged from the attainment of a cessation of perception and feeling, what does their mind incline to?

831
00:53:39,540 --> 00:53:40,020
[SPEAKER_04]: Solitude.

832
00:53:40,200 --> 00:53:40,681
[SPEAKER_04]: Seclusion.

833
00:53:42,582 --> 00:53:43,702
[SPEAKER_05]: Their mind inclines to... Dispassion.

834
00:53:43,762 --> 00:53:44,003
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

835
00:53:44,223 --> 00:53:45,443
[SPEAKER_05]: Detachment.

836
00:53:52,547 --> 00:53:53,668
[SPEAKER_05]: How many feelings are there?

837
00:53:54,968 --> 00:53:55,789
[SPEAKER_05]: There are three feelings.

838
00:53:55,989 --> 00:53:57,950
[SPEAKER_05]: Pleasant, painful and neutral.

839
00:53:59,075 --> 00:54:00,355
[SPEAKER_05]: What are these three feelings?

840
00:54:01,716 --> 00:54:05,517
[SPEAKER_05]: Anything felt physical or mental as pleasant and enjoyable.

841
00:54:06,958 --> 00:54:11,259
[SPEAKER_05]: Anything felt physical or mentally as painful or unpleasant, this is painful.

842
00:54:11,619 --> 00:54:17,421
[SPEAKER_05]: Anything felt physically or mentally as neither pleasurable nor painful, this is neutral feeling.

843
00:54:18,942 --> 00:54:22,703
[SPEAKER_05]: So, what is pleasant and what is painful in each of these feelings?

844
00:54:25,505 --> 00:54:30,008
[SPEAKER_05]: So, pleasant feeling is pleasant when it remains and painful in its ending.

845
00:54:30,068 --> 00:54:33,250
[SPEAKER_05]: Painful feeling is painful when it remains and pleasant in its ending.

846
00:54:34,210 --> 00:54:39,494
[SPEAKER_05]: Neutral feeling is pleasant when known and unpleasant when not known.

847
00:54:41,074 --> 00:54:42,195
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, we spoke about it often.

848
00:54:42,856 --> 00:54:44,236
[SPEAKER_05]: We just spoke about it the last time, didn't we?

849
00:54:44,477 --> 00:54:44,937
[SPEAKER_03]: Probably.

850
00:54:48,519 --> 00:54:52,902
[SPEAKER_05]: So, what underlying tendencies underlie each of these three feelings?

851
00:54:53,832 --> 00:55:06,181
[SPEAKER_05]: So, the underlying tendency for lust underlies pleasant feeling, the underlying tendency for resistance underlies painful feeling, the underlying tendency for ignorance underlies neutral feeling.

852
00:55:08,042 --> 00:55:15,607
[SPEAKER_05]: Do these underlying tendencies always underlie these feelings?

853
00:55:15,687 --> 00:55:16,328
[SPEAKER_05]: No, they do not.

854
00:55:19,822 --> 00:55:22,923
[SPEAKER_05]: So, what should be given up in regard to each of these three feelings?

855
00:55:23,403 --> 00:55:28,685
[SPEAKER_05]: The underlying tendency to lust should be given up when it comes to pleasant feeling.

856
00:55:28,705 --> 00:55:32,607
[SPEAKER_05]: The underlying tendency to resistance should be given up when it comes to painful feeling.

857
00:55:32,767 --> 00:55:36,188
[SPEAKER_05]: The underlying tendency to ignorance should be given up when it comes to neutral feeling.

858
00:55:39,449 --> 00:55:47,412
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, so then... but it's obviously not as... practically speaking, because what is pleasant

859
00:55:49,612 --> 00:55:51,054
[SPEAKER_03]: In a pleasant feeling.

860
00:55:51,334 --> 00:55:53,376
[SPEAKER_03]: And what is unpleasant in a pleasant feeling?

861
00:55:55,598 --> 00:55:56,359
[SPEAKER_03]: You've just read that.

862
00:55:57,020 --> 00:55:57,260
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

863
00:55:57,741 --> 00:55:59,342
[SPEAKER_05]: When it's... Change.

864
00:56:00,403 --> 00:56:00,824
[SPEAKER_03]: Changes.

865
00:56:00,884 --> 00:56:11,315
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you are then underline... underlied tendency for aversion towards the unpleasant aspect of the pleasant feeling.

866
00:56:12,962 --> 00:56:13,142
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

867
00:56:14,023 --> 00:56:20,790
[SPEAKER_03]: So, when there is a threat of change to something that's held dear and pleasant, you're averse to that.

868
00:56:21,430 --> 00:56:26,395
[SPEAKER_03]: Although it's still that same pleasant experience, now it's the aversion underlying it.

869
00:56:29,718 --> 00:56:34,743
[SPEAKER_03]: Or lust, when you equally carelessly accept it, take it for granted, want more of it.

870
00:56:38,432 --> 00:57:01,628
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's, again, none of that would be obvious, none of that would become apparent if somebody is first and foremost not acting out of their feelings, including feeling justified in trying to get rid of this lustful thought, that is obviously still regarded as pleasure, but now, because of all the other views, oh, that is, I must get rid of this, I must get rid of this, but now you're just fueling further that aversion.

871
00:57:03,820 --> 00:57:12,947
[SPEAKER_03]: So, either way, underlying tendencies remain and are being perpetuated.

872
00:57:12,967 --> 00:57:16,870
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you start with the pleasant feeling that's underlined by lust.

873
00:57:19,652 --> 00:57:21,693
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, so, but lust is a problem, I must be restrained.

874
00:57:21,934 --> 00:57:24,896
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, you're averse to it.

875
00:57:25,356 --> 00:57:25,897
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, again...

876
00:57:25,917 --> 00:57:26,457
[SPEAKER_03]: Aversion is a problem.

877
00:57:26,477 --> 00:57:26,797
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

878
00:57:26,937 --> 00:57:30,540
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, aversion is a problem.

879
00:57:30,580 --> 00:57:31,781
[SPEAKER_03]: So, how do you fuel lust?

880
00:57:32,001 --> 00:57:33,042
[SPEAKER_03]: How do you fuel aversion?

881
00:57:34,212 --> 00:57:35,513
[SPEAKER_03]: How do you stop feeling lust?

882
00:57:35,753 --> 00:57:37,174
[SPEAKER_03]: How do you stop feeling the aversion?

883
00:57:37,414 --> 00:57:39,135
[SPEAKER_04]: Acting out of it or not acting out of it.

884
00:57:39,175 --> 00:57:40,456
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

885
00:57:40,476 --> 00:57:55,246
[SPEAKER_03]: So, obviously, when you're talking about the unpleasant aspect of a pleasant feeling, that is underlined, underlied by the aversion, that will be a subtle form, subtler form of action, but it's still an action.

886
00:57:55,967 --> 00:58:02,351
[SPEAKER_03]: Aversive thoughts, aversive methods, rooted in aversion, trying to get rid of it.

887
00:58:03,844 --> 00:58:04,925
[SPEAKER_03]: That's just your aversion.

888
00:58:05,585 --> 00:58:08,126
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's why you will remain bound to that which you're still lustful for.

889
00:58:09,527 --> 00:58:15,871
[SPEAKER_03]: But if you don't feel the lust, nor you act out of aversion, you get to actually see it for what it is.

890
00:58:17,171 --> 00:58:18,412
[SPEAKER_03]: But it's unpleasant now.

891
00:58:19,913 --> 00:58:27,457
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you can't, well, abandon the underlying tendency of either lust or aversion by

892
00:58:28,717 --> 00:58:34,119
[SPEAKER_03]: The only way you can abandon it is through attending to it concurrently, as we said.

893
00:58:35,400 --> 00:58:41,822
[SPEAKER_03]: Concurrently attending to what is there a necessary basis for either your lust, or your aversion, or your actions that come out of it.

894
00:58:41,982 --> 00:58:51,365
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, it's a feeling arisen on its own, still endures, agreeable, but now I perceive a danger in any form of engagement with it.

895
00:58:51,985 --> 00:58:56,727
[SPEAKER_03]: Whether I reach for it to bring it closer, or whether I reach for it to push it away,

896
00:58:57,657 --> 00:58:58,437
[SPEAKER_03]: Reaching for it.

897
00:58:58,597 --> 00:58:59,618
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why I suffer.

898
00:58:59,778 --> 00:59:00,818
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why I get burned.

899
00:59:01,478 --> 00:59:03,099
[SPEAKER_03]: Not because the thing is there on its own.

900
00:59:03,959 --> 00:59:08,061
[SPEAKER_03]: But that will not become apparent if you haven't attended to it rightly.

901
00:59:08,081 --> 00:59:09,081
[SPEAKER_03]: Yoniso manasikāra.

902
00:59:09,821 --> 00:59:21,385
[SPEAKER_03]: If you haven't attended to it concurrently, presence of that feeling means all these permutations and aversion and lust and acting and restraining, because this thing is there.

903
00:59:22,386 --> 00:59:25,607
[SPEAKER_03]: And you get to see through all these movements and kind of

904
00:59:26,780 --> 00:59:33,345
[SPEAKER_03]: Options and jumps of the mind, you actually perceive right through it what's at the other end of it, which is an enduring feeling.

905
00:59:33,505 --> 00:59:40,690
[SPEAKER_03]: Yoniso, this whole activity that I'm being overwhelmed and restraining and acting and justifying... Oh, presence and feeling, right.

906
00:59:41,511 --> 00:59:43,272
[SPEAKER_03]: So, when that feeling is not present, all this is redundant.

907
00:59:43,332 --> 00:59:45,193
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, well, it's already redundant by seeing that.

908
00:59:46,394 --> 00:59:47,315
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's how you calm down.

909
00:59:48,230 --> 00:59:55,300
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and in the same way, you cannot free yourself from the pressure of thinking and pondering by breaking into speech, because it's a completely separate domain.

910
00:59:55,401 --> 00:59:55,721
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

911
00:59:56,542 --> 00:59:57,143
[SPEAKER_03]: And not just that.

912
00:59:57,424 --> 01:00:01,770
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, when you do break into speech, you realize, as I said, the body is speaking for you.

913
01:00:02,531 --> 01:00:03,172
[SPEAKER_03]: You are not doing it.

914
01:00:04,925 --> 01:00:10,366
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's actually analogous to wanting to get rid of the pressure of sensuality by acting out bodily.

915
01:00:11,187 --> 01:00:22,129
[SPEAKER_01]: In exactly the same way, you cannot free yourself from the pressure of thinking and pondering by breaking into speech, which is why both those domains need to be understood in order to dwell free from sensuality and unwholesome states.

916
01:00:22,169 --> 01:00:22,990
[SPEAKER_01]: And the pressure, yeah.

917
01:00:23,390 --> 01:00:23,910
[SPEAKER_01]: In the first jhāna.

918
01:00:23,930 --> 01:00:28,711
[SPEAKER_03]: You are pressured by the domain you assumed can pressure you.

919
01:00:29,311 --> 01:00:32,132
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you gave it power to pressure you and it's actually pressuring you.

920
01:00:33,214 --> 01:00:38,538
[SPEAKER_03]: Obviously, there was no first point manifested before which you didn't give it power, and then you gave it power.

921
01:00:38,578 --> 01:00:44,482
[SPEAKER_03]: You already find yourself overpowered by it, due to your own wrong notions and assumptions.

922
01:00:54,467 --> 01:01:00,649
[SPEAKER_05]: So, should these underlying... should those underlying tendencies be given up regarding all instances of these feelings?

923
01:01:01,529 --> 01:01:07,371
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it is... are there always these underlying tendencies to every single pleasant feeling?

924
01:01:08,972 --> 01:01:11,193
[SPEAKER_05]: No, there's no... not in all instances.

925
01:01:12,125 --> 01:01:25,550
[SPEAKER_05]: Take a monk who is withdrawn from sensual desires, from sensual pleasures, desires for sensual pleasures, withdrawn from unskillful qualities, enters upon the first jhāna, which has the pleasure and joy born of withdrawal.

926
01:01:26,070 --> 01:01:26,911
[SPEAKER_05]: Thinking and pondering.

927
01:01:26,931 --> 01:01:28,011
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, thinking and pondering.

928
01:01:29,071 --> 01:01:32,353
[SPEAKER_03]: With this, they give up lust and the underlying tendency to lust.

929
01:01:32,653 --> 01:01:35,634
[SPEAKER_03]: Just to, again, specify in light of what we've just been saying.

930
01:01:36,434 --> 01:01:41,556
[SPEAKER_03]: So, with quite a degree of activity of thinking and pondering,

931
01:01:42,807 --> 01:01:51,430
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not thinking and pondering, but actually without thinking and pondering, which is what contemporary notions of jhānas would generally seem to imply.

932
01:01:51,550 --> 01:01:56,132
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, yes, thinking and pondering, but then when you look at it, oh, it's no thinking, no pondering, because that's too active.

933
01:01:56,952 --> 01:02:00,393
[SPEAKER_03]: But no, no, with thinking and pondering, with verbal saṅkhara.

934
01:02:01,513 --> 01:02:04,995
[SPEAKER_03]: Pleasure and joy, born of seclusion, born of safety.

935
01:02:05,835 --> 01:02:11,897
[SPEAKER_03]: Safety from fully discerning the extent of the domains that cannot touch you, because you're not in them.

936
01:02:14,329 --> 01:02:20,093
[SPEAKER_03]: Why would you worry about what's happening in the prison, when you realize you're not in it anymore?

937
01:02:26,337 --> 01:02:29,058
[SPEAKER_03]: And you were in it before, because your assumption was keeping you in it.

938
01:02:32,961 --> 01:02:34,742
[SPEAKER_05]: So, that pleasure that is there...

939
01:02:34,762 --> 01:02:35,703
[SPEAKER_03]: It's the pleasure of safety.

940
01:02:35,863 --> 01:02:36,083
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

941
01:02:37,544 --> 01:02:38,584
[SPEAKER_05]: Can you lust after this?

942
01:02:39,185 --> 01:02:39,705
[SPEAKER_03]: Possible.

943
01:02:39,785 --> 01:02:41,506
[SPEAKER_05]: Because it's the abandonment of that lust.

944
01:02:41,526 --> 01:02:41,686
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

945
01:02:43,127 --> 01:02:44,088
[SPEAKER_03]: Do you need to get rid of it?

946
01:02:44,448 --> 01:02:44,628
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

947
01:02:46,169 --> 01:02:47,329
[SPEAKER_03]: Would you fear if it changes?

948
01:02:47,629 --> 01:02:52,011
[SPEAKER_03]: No, because you know that the change will have to be within the separate domains that you're safe from now.

949
01:02:52,872 --> 01:02:54,632
[SPEAKER_03]: Thus, there will be no aversion towards it either.

950
01:02:56,573 --> 01:02:57,914
[SPEAKER_05]: No fear of that change.

951
01:02:57,934 --> 01:02:58,254
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

952
01:02:58,714 --> 01:02:59,635
[SPEAKER_03]: No fear, no aversion.

953
01:03:01,695 --> 01:03:04,737
[SPEAKER_05]: And then, so, what about a painful feeling?

954
01:03:05,377 --> 01:03:09,059
[SPEAKER_05]: Is there an underlying tendency to aversion towards all painful feelings?

955
01:03:09,459 --> 01:03:09,579
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

956
01:03:10,533 --> 01:03:16,996
[SPEAKER_05]: It says, take a monk who thinks, oh, when will I enter and remain in that same dimension that the noble ones enter and remain in?

957
01:03:18,717 --> 01:03:24,059
[SPEAKER_05]: And nursing such a longing for the supreme liberations gives rise to sadness.

958
01:03:25,500 --> 01:03:31,503
[SPEAKER_05]: With this, they give up the resistance and underlying tendency to resistance does not lie underneath that.

959
01:03:32,243 --> 01:03:33,143
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, they give up the aversion.

960
01:03:33,303 --> 01:03:36,065
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, they give up that resistance, they give up.

961
01:03:37,820 --> 01:03:40,121
[SPEAKER_05]: Conversion towards that painful feeling of sadness.

962
01:03:41,661 --> 01:03:48,362
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's a pretty much painful feeling they were standing for.

963
01:03:48,402 --> 01:03:50,223
[SPEAKER_03]: The domain that the noble ones dwell in.

964
01:03:51,083 --> 01:03:52,463
[SPEAKER_03]: You just needed to stop resisting it.

965
01:03:54,364 --> 01:04:00,105
[SPEAKER_03]: And when he finally stops resisting it, he's in the domain of the noble ones that are dwelling peacefully.

966
01:04:01,185 --> 01:04:03,846
[SPEAKER_03]: Because now he's not averse towards it.

967
01:04:07,487 --> 01:04:19,611
[SPEAKER_05]: So, and then, in terms of neutral feeling, is that underlying tendency towards ignorance, does it underlie all neutral feeling?

968
01:04:19,631 --> 01:04:32,356
[SPEAKER_05]: It says, no, take a monk who, abandoning pleasure and pain, and ending former happiness and sadness, enters upon the fourth jhāna, without pleasure or pain, with pure equanimity and recollection.

969
01:04:33,056 --> 01:04:37,058
[SPEAKER_05]: With this, they give up ignorance, and the underlying tendency to ignorance does not lie.

970
01:04:38,664 --> 01:04:39,184
[SPEAKER_03]: Under that.

971
01:04:47,790 --> 01:04:49,392
[SPEAKER_05]: So, these are the last questions.

972
01:04:50,793 --> 01:04:53,695
[SPEAKER_05]: What is the counterpart of pleasant feeling?

973
01:04:56,497 --> 01:04:56,697
Painful.

974
01:04:57,838 --> 01:05:03,182
[SPEAKER_05]: What is the counterpart of pleasant feeling?

975
01:05:04,803 --> 01:05:05,163
[SPEAKER_05]: What is it?

976
01:05:06,905 --> 01:05:07,485
[SPEAKER_03]: Painful feeling.

977
01:05:09,550 --> 01:05:10,810
[SPEAKER_03]: What's the Pali word for counterpart?

978
01:05:11,430 --> 01:05:12,251
[SPEAKER_05]: It is counterpart.

979
01:05:12,271 --> 01:05:12,631
[SPEAKER_05]: That's it.

980
01:05:13,211 --> 01:05:14,331
[SPEAKER_05]: So, I don't quite understand the...

981
01:05:14,811 --> 01:05:16,552
[SPEAKER_05]: It's like, what is the counterpart of pleasant feeling?

982
01:05:16,572 --> 01:05:17,332
[SPEAKER_05]: What is the opposite?

983
01:05:17,672 --> 01:05:18,352
[SPEAKER_05]: That's what he's asking.

984
01:05:18,372 --> 01:05:19,732
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the answer to the feelings.

985
01:05:20,292 --> 01:05:20,552
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

986
01:05:21,233 --> 01:05:22,053
[SPEAKER_05]: So, painful feeling.

987
01:05:23,053 --> 01:05:25,213
[SPEAKER_05]: What is the counterpart of painful feeling?

988
01:05:26,114 --> 01:05:26,734
[SPEAKER_00]: Pleasant feeling.

989
01:05:27,494 --> 01:05:29,794
[SPEAKER_05]: What is the counterpart of neutral feeling?

990
01:05:35,536 --> 01:05:36,976
[SPEAKER_03]: What's the counterpart of neutral feeling?

991
01:05:38,517 --> 01:05:41,539
[SPEAKER_05]: What is the opposite of neutral feeling?

992
01:05:49,162 --> 01:05:49,522
[SPEAKER_05]: Ignorance.

993
01:05:50,763 --> 01:05:55,805
[SPEAKER_05]: I don't know how that's the answer.

994
01:05:56,826 --> 01:05:57,126
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

995
01:05:57,646 --> 01:05:59,207
[SPEAKER_03]: What is the counterpart of neutral feeling?

996
01:05:59,607 --> 01:05:59,927
[SPEAKER_03]: Ignorance.

997
01:06:00,828 --> 01:06:05,470
[SPEAKER_03]: Presence of pain, of pleasant feeling counterpart would be pain, when that pleasure changes.

998
01:06:06,790 --> 01:06:11,532
[SPEAKER_03]: The presence of painful feeling counterpart is pleasant, because it will have to disappear, the pain.

999
01:06:11,872 --> 01:06:12,992
[SPEAKER_03]: So, then, you experience pleasure.

1000
01:06:13,252 --> 01:06:14,092
[SPEAKER_03]: The presence is outward.

1001
01:06:14,112 --> 01:06:14,833
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like the other...

1002
01:06:15,213 --> 01:06:15,713
[SPEAKER_00]: The other end of it.

1003
01:06:15,733 --> 01:06:16,273
[SPEAKER_00]: The other end of it.

1004
01:06:16,353 --> 01:06:16,733
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

1005
01:06:16,773 --> 01:06:17,874
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I was going to say.

1006
01:06:17,894 --> 01:06:18,474
[SPEAKER_00]: The other side.

1007
01:06:18,714 --> 01:06:19,574
[SPEAKER_03]: The other side of it.

1008
01:06:19,694 --> 01:06:20,354
[SPEAKER_03]: The other...

1009
01:06:20,374 --> 01:06:22,855
[SPEAKER_03]: So, presence of neutral feeling, it's ignorance.

1010
01:06:22,875 --> 01:06:23,395
[SPEAKER_00]: It's ignorance.

1011
01:06:24,596 --> 01:06:28,537
[SPEAKER_00]: No knowledge.

1012
01:06:28,717 --> 01:06:31,418
[SPEAKER_05]: The other side of neutral feeling is ignorance.

1013
01:06:35,058 --> 01:06:35,898
[SPEAKER_05]: I still don't get that.

1014
01:06:35,938 --> 01:06:36,539
[SPEAKER_02]: Simultaneous.

1015
01:06:36,559 --> 01:06:40,480
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, because you don't start neutral.

1016
01:06:41,880 --> 01:06:45,162
[SPEAKER_03]: When an enlightened mind experiences neutral feeling, it's being not understood.

1017
01:06:45,702 --> 01:06:47,863
[SPEAKER_03]: That is the presence there, of that neutral feeling.

1018
01:06:49,123 --> 01:06:50,023
[SPEAKER_03]: The counterpart of it.

1019
01:06:51,804 --> 01:06:52,324
[SPEAKER_03]: Not after.

1020
01:06:52,744 --> 01:06:53,465
[SPEAKER_03]: At the same time.

1021
01:06:55,765 --> 01:06:57,386
[SPEAKER_03]: So, what is the counterpart of ignorance?

1022
01:06:58,006 --> 01:06:59,667
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, non-ignorance.

1023
01:07:01,212 --> 01:07:05,413
[SPEAKER_03]: So now, when ignorance is seen as present, well, see, you stopped ignoring it.

1024
01:07:06,113 --> 01:07:07,593
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the other side.

1025
01:07:07,733 --> 01:07:09,173
[SPEAKER_03]: It's another simultaneous thing now.

1026
01:07:11,234 --> 01:07:22,336
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you couldn't... you could only answer that question, like, what is the counterpart or the other end of neutral feeling, if you had already kind of understood the counterpart of ignorance.

1027
01:07:22,396 --> 01:07:22,716
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

1028
01:07:22,996 --> 01:07:25,177
[SPEAKER_00]: Otherwise, that's just all invisible, basically.

1029
01:07:26,717 --> 01:07:27,757
[SPEAKER_05]: To some extent, and then...

1030
01:07:28,423 --> 01:07:33,444
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, you could start, sort of, carving it from the inside, but it will take a while.

1031
01:07:35,385 --> 01:07:36,665
[SPEAKER_05]: So, what is the counterpart of ignorance?

1032
01:07:37,145 --> 01:07:39,266
[SPEAKER_05]: Is it non-ignorance or knowledge?

1033
01:07:39,786 --> 01:07:41,466
[SPEAKER_05]: What is the counterpart of knowledge?

1034
01:07:42,626 --> 01:07:42,827
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

1035
01:07:42,927 --> 01:07:51,329
[SPEAKER_03]: So, what is... what is the present end of present knowledge?

1036
01:07:51,809 --> 01:07:52,729
[SPEAKER_03]: What is the other present?

1037
01:07:54,554 --> 01:07:56,981
[SPEAKER_04]: I can't think of a better word, actually.

1038
01:07:57,743 --> 01:08:00,913
[SPEAKER_04]: It's like liberation and more... Dispassion?

1039
01:08:02,304 --> 01:08:02,904
[SPEAKER_05]: Liberation.

1040
01:08:03,344 --> 01:08:03,644
[SPEAKER_05]: Freedom.

1041
01:08:04,104 --> 01:08:04,444
[SPEAKER_03]: Freedom.

1042
01:08:06,065 --> 01:08:12,066
[SPEAKER_03]: Presence of knowledge means freedom is present, liberation is present, dispassion is present.

1043
01:08:12,186 --> 01:08:24,168
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if the knowledge is present sufficiently long, its counterpart will be equally present sufficiently long enough for you to fully then see liberation, freedom as that which is present now.

1044
01:08:24,388 --> 01:08:25,708
[SPEAKER_03]: So, what is the counterpart of that?

1045
01:08:26,208 --> 01:08:26,628
[SPEAKER_05]: Of freedom.

1046
01:08:26,768 --> 01:08:26,929
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

1047
01:08:28,129 --> 01:08:29,369
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, that's complete nibbāna.

1048
01:08:30,315 --> 01:08:30,735
[SPEAKER_05]: Nibbāna.

1049
01:08:32,075 --> 01:08:34,076
[SPEAKER_05]: So, what is the counterpart of nibbāna?

1050
01:08:34,096 --> 01:08:35,956
[SPEAKER_03]: What is the counterpart of that nibbāna?

1051
01:08:37,196 --> 01:08:39,117
[SPEAKER_03]: That designation does not reach that.

1052
01:08:39,597 --> 01:08:45,298
[SPEAKER_05]: And the nun, she answers Visakha, your question goes too far.

1053
01:08:46,898 --> 01:08:48,758
[SPEAKER_01]: You couldn't figure out the limits of questions.

1054
01:08:49,318 --> 01:08:52,819
[SPEAKER_05]: For nibbāna is the culmination, the destination, and the goal is holy life.

1055
01:08:55,079 --> 01:08:58,140
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, also nibbāna is the undetermined, so how can it have an opposite?

1056
01:08:58,759 --> 01:08:59,099
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

1057
01:08:59,740 --> 01:09:01,041
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that is the end of it.

1058
01:09:04,443 --> 01:09:23,337
[SPEAKER_04]: This... that... the counterpart of knowledge being freedom, to me, also points to this... this... the fact that... the suffering is the ultimate... coming out of suffering is like the ultimate...

1059
01:09:24,278 --> 01:09:26,899
[SPEAKER_04]: So, what is the ultimate knowledge?

1060
01:09:27,119 --> 01:09:28,740
[SPEAKER_04]: What is the ultimate counterpart?

1061
01:09:28,760 --> 01:09:31,401
[SPEAKER_04]: It's freedom from suffering.

1062
01:09:40,525 --> 01:09:46,150
[SPEAKER_04]: Intention that anyone can have that isn't rooted in wanting to come out of suffering, wanting freedom.

1063
01:09:46,290 --> 01:09:56,659
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, I think, basically, as long as, as long as the... just to highlight, to repeat, the counterpart, its simultaneous presence, it should not be understood as of what comes later.

1064
01:09:57,900 --> 01:10:03,665
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's like, as I said, painful feeling is implied in current pleasure.

1065
01:10:05,146 --> 01:10:09,488
[SPEAKER_03]: Because, current pleasure we have to change, and there will have to be, then, felt painfully.

1066
01:10:09,728 --> 01:10:10,688
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's already painful.

1067
01:10:10,948 --> 01:10:18,472
[SPEAKER_03]: Knowledge, that just presence of that pleasure to an ignorant mind, it already implies pain.

1068
01:10:18,512 --> 01:10:18,752
[SPEAKER_03]: Same.

1069
01:10:19,392 --> 01:10:23,194
[SPEAKER_03]: Presence of pain already implies pleasure, because it will have to end one way or the other.

1070
01:10:26,475 --> 01:10:28,396
[SPEAKER_03]: Neutral feeling implies ignoring.

1071
01:10:29,876 --> 01:10:32,397
[SPEAKER_03]: Now you see ignoring, well, that implies non-ignoring.

1072
01:10:34,754 --> 01:10:35,374
[SPEAKER_05]: Not ignoring.

1073
01:10:36,755 --> 01:10:38,075
[SPEAKER_05]: You see distraction.

1074
01:10:38,295 --> 01:10:39,556
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, you could say that.

1075
01:10:40,216 --> 01:10:44,278
[SPEAKER_04]: And like the eightfold path, this isn't linear?

1076
01:10:44,618 --> 01:10:45,118
[SPEAKER_04]: No, no.

1077
01:10:45,338 --> 01:10:56,983
[SPEAKER_03]: It's as you said, it's the three aspects of one development, which is development of dispassion and freedom from suffering.

1078
01:10:57,043 --> 01:10:57,403
[SPEAKER_03]: That's it.

